<urn:atm:triage:finding/RBP-F003>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RBP-F003" ;
  triage:title "OrderedMap<K,V>(Vec<(K,V)>) is deserialized 'in document order' with no stated duplicate-k" ;
  triage:description "OrderedMap<K,V>(Vec<(K,V)>) is deserialized 'in document order' with no stated duplicate-key rule, so a body with repeated question or criteria keys is representable and ambiguous. JevQuestion has public variant fields and derived Deserialize, so the '2..=255' and '2..=10' limits in comments are not enforced on that path. CheckRequest derives Deserialize, so to_canonical (used by input_sha256 recovery) can run on an unvalidated body. JevState::Object and the response usage/extra maps are serde_json::Map; the plan says 'no serde_json feature', so these are key-sorted. The 'key order is document order' and 'extra verbatim' claims and the state-object roundtrip are not true for unsorted keys. The key-ordering test uses already-sorted keys (context, inputs, metadata), so it cannot detect this. Recommendation: Have the OrderedMap visitor reject duplicate keys (INPUT_INVALID or RESPONSE_INVALID, with a fix hint). Enforce criteria counts in a validating constructor or try_from used by both from_canonical and Deserialize. Use OrderedMap<String, Value> for JevState::Object. Either state that nested Value objects are key-sorted and the claim is limited accordingly, or drop the 'verbatim' wording. Use an unsorted-key case in test_state_forms_roundtrip and the key-ordering test." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-a-refine" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "RBP" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-11-07T22:47:43Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:A1-PLAN67 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-11-07T23:55:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
